+# Send pickup reminders by SMS
+
+For volunteers considering a change to pickup reminders, including switching to email to save money. Keep SMS:
+the email trial increased missed pickups and wasted fresh food, and returning to SMS restored the earlier pickup
+rate.
+
+## Context
+
+SMS pickup reminders cost €40 a month. Email was tried from April to June 2025 to avoid that cost. According to the
+provider's report, as recorded in [issue 52](../../notes/issue-52.md), 34% of reminder emails were never opened.
+Missed pickups rose from 6% to 13% of pantry orders. Each missed pickup leaves a packed bag whose fresh food goes
+in the bin by closing time.
+
+The pantry lead reported that many members share one phone within the family and never check email, but see SMS.
+Reminders switched back to SMS on July 1, 2025; missed pickups were back to 6% by August. In issue 52, the third
+proposal that year to switch to email, the maintainer confirmed that SMS would stay.
+
+The choice must account for whether members see reminders, the missed pickups and food waste that follow, and
+the cost of sending them.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Email**: avoids the €40 monthly SMS cost. Rejected because the trial left 34% of reminder emails unopened and
+  missed pickups rose to 13%, wasting packed fresh food; many members do not check email.
+- **SMS**: costs €40 a month, but reaches members who see texts and do not check email. Chosen because missed
+  pickups returned to 6% after SMS resumed.
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep pickup reminders on SMS. The observed improvement in pickups and the reduction in wasted fresh food justify
+the sending cost over email. [The reminder implementation](../../larder/reminders.py) selects SMS with `CHANNEL`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The pantry continues paying €40 a month for SMS, accepting that expense to reduce missed pickups and food waste.
+Future proposals to change channels should address the trial's results and members' ability to see reminders,
+as well as sending cost.
